  "content": {
    "id": "p29-q7-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "7",
    "prompt": "The number $\\frac{1}{11}$, written as a decimal, is $0.\\overline{09}$, which repeats every 2 digits after the decimal point. No smaller number of digits repeats. Find all positive integer values of $n$ for which the decimal expansion of $\\frac{1}{n}$ repeats immediately after the decimal point with shortest repeating block of length 2.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": {
        "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
        "version": 1,
        "blocks": [
          {
            "id": "p29-q7-short-result",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "fontSize": null,
            "align": "left",
            "spaceBefore": 0,
            "spaceAfter": 2,
            "lineHeight": 1.4,
            "indent": 0,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "math",
                "latex": "n=11,\\ 33,\\ 99",
                "display": false
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "p29-q7-short-method",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "fontSize": null,
            "align": "left",
            "spaceBefore": 0,
            "spaceAfter": 2,
            "lineHeight": 1.4,
            "indent": 0,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Multiplying by 100 and subtracting shows that n divides 99. Exclude denominators whose decimals have a one-digit repeating block.",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "worked": "Let $x=\\frac{1}{n}=0.\\overline{ab}$, where $A=10a+b$ is the integer represented by the two-digit block, allowing a leading zero.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}100x&=A+0.\\overline{ab}\\\\x&=0.\\overline{ab}\\\\99x&=A\\\\\\frac{99}{n}&=A\\end{align*}$$\n\nThus $n$ divides $99$, so its possible values are $1,3,9,11,33,99$. The values $1,3,9$ give an integer or a decimal with a one-digit repeating block, so they do not qualify.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{1}{11}&=0.\\overline{09}\\\\\\frac{1}{33}&=0.\\overline{03}\\\\\\frac{1}{99}&=0.\\overline{01}\\end{align*}$$\n\nEach remaining block contains two different digits, so its shortest period is 2. Therefore $n=11,33,99$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 122
  },
